  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence — Compromise Host Software Binary (Baseline Tamper)", "technique": "T1554", "what_happened": "At 2027-08-14 02:11:07 CEST, Wazuh FIM logged rule 550 'Integrity checksum changed' on WIN-BACKUP-07 for the monitored binary C:\\Program Files\\BackupAgent\\agent.exe. The syscheck event recorded a SHA-256 transition: the previously-baselined hash a3f1c9d2e8b47650f1029e4477bc5521d9a0e3f8124b6677aa90cc1de2f43381 changed to 7e2b88c401aa93fd56710e9bd3c2a45f0089ef6612d7740bb5512ace99d01a77. The binary sits in a trusted, monitored Program Files path — no approved change-window or patch ticket was open for the BackupAgent product at the time of the event.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh syscheck rule 550 fired with syscheck.path=C:\\Program Files\\BackupAgent\\agent.exe and syscheck.event=modified. The univariate signature: a monitored Program Files binary changes hash outside any maintenance window. This is PDF artefact A (Binary Integrity Violation: HashStatus=Changed).", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T02:11:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence — Compromise Host Software Binary (Hash Drift Confirmed)", "technique": "T1554", "what_happened": "At 02:13:52 CEST, Wazuh custom rule 92200 'FIM: monitored binary hash drift' fired on WIN-BACKUP-07, correlating the rule-550 checksum change against the FIM gold-baseline store and confirming the new hash 7e2b88c401aa93fd56710e9bd3c2a45f0089ef6612d7740bb5512ace99d01a77 is NOT a known-good vendor build. The file size grew by ~14 KB versus the baseline — consistent with code-cave / appended-section injection (malicious code appended to a legitimate binary, the canonical T1554 backdooring pattern).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92200 (level 13) correlates syscheck.sha256_after against the approved-build allowlist. syscheck.path unchanged, syscheck.event=modified, the post-change hash absent from the vendor allowlist. This is PDF artefact A + E (hash drift with no verified update source).", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T02:13:52+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Defense Evasion — Compromise Host Software Binary (Signature Invalidation)", "technique": "T1554", "what_happened": "At 02:15:20 CEST, Wazuh custom rule 92201 'Authenticode: trusted-path binary unsigned' fired on WIN-BACKUP-07 for C:\\Program Files\\BackupAgent\\agent.exe. The Authenticode signature that was Valid at the last baseline is now Invalid — the appended bytes broke the digital signature. A binary in a trusted Program Files path that was signed and is now unsigned is the textbook tampered-trusted-software signal. No new Run keys and no new Scheduled Tasks were created — the attacker poisoned what already exists rather than adding new persistence artefacts (PDF artefact D: stealth persistence).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92201 (level 13) evaluates the Authenticode status of monitored Program Files binaries; status flipped Valid→Invalid for syscheck.path=C:\\Program Files\\BackupAgent\\agent.exe. This is PDF artefact A (Signature=Invalid) + D (No New Persistence Entries).", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T02:15:20+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Command and Control — Application Layer Protocol (Trusted Binary Beacon)", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 02:18:33 CEST, ~3.5 minutes after the signature-invalidation alert, Sysmon Event 3 logged an outbound network connection from C:\\Program Files\\BackupAgent\\agent.exe on WIN-BACKUP-07 (sourceIp 10.108.57.41) to destinationIp 10.108.57.184 on destinationPort 443. The drifted backup-agent binary — a process that legitimately only talks to the on-prem backup repository — initiated outbound HTTPS to an unknown internal-range endpoint that is NOT a sanctioned backup target. This matches PDF artefact C (Unexpected Behaviour: agent.exe → Outbound HTTPS → Unknown IP) and PDF artefact F (Follow-on Activity: NextAction=CommandAndControl). The connection repeated at 02:21:48, suggesting a beacon interval.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3 on WIN-BACKUP-07 with data.win.eventdata.image=C:\\Program Files\\BackupAgent\\agent.exe + data.win.eventdata.destinationIp=10.108.57.184 + data.win.eventdata.destinationPort=443. Linkage to steps 1-3: the SAME tampered binary path is both the FIM-drift subject and the network-initiating process. Correlation rule 'Tampered trusted binary initiating outbound connection' chained the syscheck drift to the Sysmon network event by syscheck.path == data.win.eventdata.image.", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T02:18:33+0200"}
  ],
